var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Optical-Propagation-Methods","page":"Optical Propagation Methods","text":"Here are some useful optical propagation methods.","title":"Optical Propagation Methods"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Fresnel–Kirchhoff-propagation","page":"Optical Propagation Methods","text":"fresnelkirchhoff evaluates a spatial zone-mask convolution using zero-padded FFTs. For normally incident illumination between parallel planes, the kernel retains the spherical propagation distance and Kirchhoff obliquity factor:\n\nh(xy) = frace^iks2ilambda sleft(1+fracdsright)\nqquad s=sqrtd^2+x^2+y^2 qquad k=frac2pilambda\n\nThe input is zero outside its sampled aperture, and the output covers the same window. Sample coordinates follow the existing light-field convention: x[i] = -lx/2 + (i-1)*lx/n, and likewise for y. Sampling must resolve the phase of the kernel. This finite-aperture integral requires sufficient sampling as the distance approaches zero; the special case d == 0 returns a copy. It does not impose periodic boundaries.\n\nusing OpticalPropagation, Unitful\n\naperture = MonoLightField2D(ones(32, 24),\n    wavelength=633u\"nm\", size=(0.1u\"mm\", 0.08u\"mm\"))\ndiffracted = fresnelkirchhoff(aperture, 20u\"mm\")\nsize(diffracted), diffracted.size\n((32, 24), (0.0001 m, 8.0e-5 m))\n\nFor a spherical reference surface, set radius to the signed curvature radius of the incident wave. Positive values describe divergence; negative values describe convergence. Data contain the actual complex amplitude on the cap. The output cap is concentric and its projected widths scale by 1 + d/radius. Propagation to or through the common centre is unsupported.\n\nspherical = fresnelkirchhoff(aperture, 20u\"mm\"; radius=100u\"mm\")\nspherical.size\n(0.00012 m, 9.6e-5 m)\n\nThe finite-radius mode implements the tangent-grid approximation motivated by Hudson's Eqs. (1)–(5), with the surface-area Jacobian applied to the input. It computes the zone mask from exact on-axis spherical geometry; translating this mask across the projected grid approximates rotation on the reference sphere. Use small caps relative to the radius. This grid approximation is not an exact integral between arbitrary spherical points.\n\nFor displacement radius rho=sqrtx^2+y^2, input radius R, and c=sqrt1-rho^2R^2, the kernel uses\n\nz_s=R(1-c) qquad\ns=sqrtrho^2+(d+z_s)^2 qquad\ncoschi=fracdc-z_ss qquad\nh=frace^iks2ilambda s(1+coschi)\n\nThe quadrature weight at each input sample is Delta xDelta yc using that sample's distance from the optical axis. In the limit Rtoinfty, these expressions reduce to the parallel-plane kernel above. The constant reference-wave factor in Hudson's Eq. (1) is already contained in the input amplitude; it is not multiplied into the result a second time.\n\nReference: Jerome A. Hudson, “Fresnel-Kirchhoff diffraction in optical systems: an approximate computational algorithm”, Applied Optics 23, 2292–2295 (1984). The implementation uses the derivation in the public publisher preview.","title":"Fresnel–Kirchhoff propagation"},{"category":"function","location":"methods/#OpticalPropagation.angularspectrum","page":"Optical Propagation Methods","text":"angularspectrum(Uin, d, λ, lx, ly)\n\ncalculate the propagation light field based on the angular spectrum.\n\nArguments\n\nUin::AbstractArray{<:Number,2}: Complex array of input complex amplitude.\nd::Real: Distance to propagate in metres.\nλ::Real: Wavelength of light to propagate.\nlx::Real: The physical size of the input data along the x-axis.\nly::Real: The physical size of the input data along the y-axis.\n\nReturns\n\n::Array{<:Number,2}: Complex amplitude data after propagation.\n\n\n\n\n\nangularspectrum(Uin::MonoLightField2D, d::Unitful.Length) -> MonoLightField2D\n\ncalculate the propagation light field for MonoLightField2D based on the angular spectrum.\n\n\n\n\n\nangularspectrum(d)\n\nrepresent an angularspectrum propagation functor with the specified distance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpticalPropagation.angularspectrum"},{"category":"function","location":"methods/#OpticalPropagation.fresnel1","page":"Optical Propagation Methods","text":"fresnel1(Uin, d, λ, lx, ly)\n\ncalculate the propagation light field based on the Fresnel diffraction with single Fourier transform.\n\nArguments\n\nUin::AbstractArray{<:Number,2}: Complex array of input complex amplitude.\nd::Real: Distance to propagate in metres.\nλ::Real: Wavelength of light to propagate.\nlx::Real: The physical size of the input data along the x-axis.\nly::Real: The physical size of the input data along the y-axis.\n\nReturns\n\n(Uout, (lxo, lyo))\n\nUout::Array{<:Number,2}: Complex amplitude data after propagation.\nlxo::Real: The physical size of the diffraction data along the x-axis.\nlyo::Real: The physical size of the diffraction data along the y-axis.\n\n\n\n\n\nfresnel1(Uin::MonoLightField2D, d::Unitful.Length) -> MonoLightField2D\n\ncalculate the propagation light field for MonoLightField2D based on the Fresnel diffraction with single Fourier transform.\n\n\n\n\n\nfresnel1(d)\n\nrepresent a fresnel1 propagation functor with the specified distance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpticalPropagation.fresnel1"},{"category":"function","location":"methods/#OpticalPropagation.fresnel2","page":"Optical Propagation Methods","text":"fresnel2(Uin, d, λ, lx, ly)\n\ncalculate the propagation light field based on the Fresnel diffraction with double Fourier transform.\n\nArguments\n\nUin::AbstractArray{<:Number,2}: Complex array of input complex amplitude.\nd::Real: Distance to propagate in metres.\nλ::Real: Wavelength of light to propagate.\nlx::Real: The physical size of the input data along the x-axis.\nly::Real: The physical size of the input data along the y-axis.\n\nReturns\n\n::Array{<:Number,2}: Complex amplitude data after propagation.\n\n\n\n\n\nfresnel2(Uin::MonoLightField2D, d::Unitful.Length) -> MonoLightField2D\n\ncalculate the propagation light field for MonoLightField2D based on the Fresnel diffraction with double Fourier transform.\n\n\n\n\n\nfresnel2(d)\n\nrepresent a fresnel2 propagation functor with the specified distance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpticalPropagation.fresnel2"},{"category":"function","location":"methods/#OpticalPropagation.fresnelkirchhoff","page":"Optical Propagation Methods","text":"fresnelkirchhoff(Uin, d, λ, lx, ly; radius=Inf)\n\nPropagate a sampled scalar field with a Fresnel–Kirchhoff zone-mask convolution, following Hudson's spherical-reference-surface approximation [1]. All lengths in this array interface are in metres. The result is a Matrix{ComplexF64} with the same number of samples as Uin; input samples outside the array are zero.\n\nd is a finite, nonnegative axial distance; λ, lx, and ly are finite and positive. lx and ly are the input widths along the first and second array dimensions, with sample pitches lx/size(Uin, 1) and ly/size(Uin, 2). Zero distance returns an independent complex copy of the input.\n\nBy default, the input and output are parallel planes with the same physical size, and normally incident illumination is assumed. The impulse response is exp(2πim*s/λ) * (1 + d/s) / (2im*λ*s), where s = sqrt(d^2 + x^2 + y^2). The FFTs are padded to compute a linear convolution without periodic wraparound.\n\nA finite, nonzero radius specifies a signed input reference-sphere radius: positive for a diverging wave and negative for a converging wave. The output sphere is concentric, with signed radius radius + d. Its projected physical size is (1 + d/radius) .* (lx, ly). Propagation to or through the common centre is unsupported. The data give the actual complex amplitude on the input cap, including its reference-wave amplitude and phase. The returned data similarly give the actual amplitude on the output cap.\n\nThe grid is projected perpendicularly from the tangent plane onto the sphere, with coordinates x[i] = -lx/2 + (i-1)*lx/n and similarly for y, as in the light-field plotting recipe. The surface-area Jacobian is included. Both the input and the entire displacement grid must fit inside a hemisphere. Translation on this grid approximates rotation on the sphere, so use caps small compared with abs(radius). The oscillatory zone mask must also be adequately sampled; zero padding does not correct undersampling or aperture truncation.\n\n[1] J. A. Hudson, \"Fresnel-Kirchhoff diffraction in optical systems: an approximate computational algorithm,\" Applied Optics 23, 2292–2295 (1984), https://doi.org/10.1364/AO.23.002292, Eqs. (1)–(5).\n\n\n\n\n\nfresnelkirchhoff(Uin::MonoLightField2D, d::Unitful.Length;\n                 radius::Unitful.Length=Inf*u\"m\") -> MonoLightField2D\n\nPropagate a monochromatic light field with fresnelkirchhoff. A finite signed radius selects a spherical reference surface and scales the output physical size by 1 + d/radius. The wavelength is preserved.\n\n\n\n\n\nfresnelkirchhoff(d; kwargs...)\n\nReturn a propagation functor with fixed distance and optional reference radius. For example, fresnelkirchhoff(1u\"cm\")(field) propagates a light field by 1 cm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpticalPropagation.fresnelkirchhoff"},{"category":"section","location":"lightfield2d/#2D-Light-Field","page":"2D Light Field","text":"The 2-dimensional light field data types provide a more convenient way to represent the light field in the plane space and can be used with our optical propagation methods.","title":"2D Light Field"},{"category":"section","location":"lightfield2d/#Work-with-Plots.jl","page":"2D Light Field","text":"The 2-dimensional light field data types can work with Plots.jl, so it will be more convenient when we want to visualize a light field. For example,\n\nusing Unitful\nusing Plots\n\ngauss = MonoLightField2D(\n    [exp(-(x^2+y^2)/6400) for x in -200:199, y in -200:199],\n    wavelength=632.8u\"nm\",\n    size=(1u\"mm\", 1u\"mm\")\n)\nplot(gauss)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=1}","title":"Work with Plots.jl"},{"category":"type","location":"lightfield2d/#OpticalPropagation.MonoLightField2D","page":"2D Light Field","text":"MonoLightField2D\n\nMonoLightField2D is used to represent a 2-dimensional monochromatic light field.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Unitful\n\njulia> MonoLightField2D([1 2; 3 4],wavelength=632.8u\"nm\",size=(1u\"mm\",1u\"mm\"))\nMonoLightField2D:\n    wavelength: 6.328e-7 m\n    physical size: (0.001 m, 0.001 m)\n    complex amplitude data:\n2×2 Matrix{ComplexF64}:\n 1.0+0.0im  2.0+0.0im\n 3.0+0.0im  4.0+0.0im\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpticalPropagation.MonoLightField2D"},{"category":"section","location":"#OpticalPropagation.jl","page":"Home","text":"","title":"OpticalPropagation.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"First, add the package\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"OpticalPropagation\")\n\nTo use some light field data types, you need to add Unitful.jl\n\nPkg.add(\"Unitful\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Basic-Examples","page":"Home","text":"Draw a cross hole and its diffraction pattern:\n\nusing OpticalPropagation\nusing Unitful\nusing Plots\n\ncross = MonoLightField2D(\n    [Int((abs(x)<10 && abs(y)<50) || (abs(x)<50 && abs(y)<10)) for y in -200:199, x in -200:199],\n    wavelength=632.8u\"nm\",\n    size=(1u\"mm\",1u\"mm\")\n)\npatt = angularspectrum(cross, 1u\"cm\")\n\nplot(\n    plot(cross, size=(420,400)),\n    plot(patt, size=(420,400)),\n    size=(840,400)\n)\nPlot{Plots.GRBackend() n=2}","title":"Basic Examples"},{"category":"module","location":"#OpticalPropagation","page":"Home","text":"A package of useful optical propagation methods and simple 2-dimensional light field data types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OpticalPropagation"}]
}
